                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Single-cell foundation models. Geneformer (Theodoris et al., 2023) introduced a rank-encoded BERT-style transformer trained with masked-language-modeling on  $ \\approx $ 30M cells. scGPT (Cui et al., 2024) extended this to generative pretraining over 33M CELLxGENE Census cells with binned expression values. UCE (Rosen et al., 2023) departs from gene-token approaches entirely, using ESM2 protein embeddings (Lin et al., 2023) as the input tokens. scFoundation (Hao et al., 2024) and several other models (Boiarsky et al., 2023) have followed in rapid succession. All draw heavily on the same public scRNA-seq repositories.",
            "block_bbox": [
                124,
                682,
                1065,
                874
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    124.0,
                    682.0
                ],
                [
                    1065.0,
                    682.0
                ],
                [
                    1065.0,
                    874.0
                ],
                [
                    124.0,
                    874.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Benchmark contamination in NLP and vision. Contamination of benchmarks by pretraining data is a well-known concern in NLP and computer vision. Magar and Schwartz (2022) formalized the distinction between memorization and exploitation in NLP benchmark contamination, and Brown et al. (2020); Sainz et al. (2023) documented overlap rates between GPT-class training corpora and downstream evaluations. Carlini et al. (2022) provides the methodological foundation for loss-based membership inference (MIA), which we adapt to the scFM setting. The single-cell community has begun to discuss the issue informally (Kedzierska et al., 2025), but no automated per-cell audit method has been published.",
            "block_bbox": [
                124,
                911,
                1066,
                1131
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    124.0,
                    911.0
                ],
                [
                    1066.0,
                    911.0
                ],
                [
                    1066.0,
                    1131.0
                ],
                [
                    124.0,
                    1131.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Integration benchmarks and silhouette-based metrics. The scIB benchmark (Luecken et al., 2022) evaluates integration methods using a basket of metrics including average silhouette width (ASW), adjusted Rand index (ARI), normalized mutual information (NMI), and graph connectivity. Recent zero-shot evaluations of scFMs (Boiarsky et al., 2023; Kedzierska et al., 2025) have used the same metrics, but without auditing pretraining exposure. Boiarsky et al. (2023) reported underwhelming zero-shot performance from foundation models across several scIB tasks but did not attribute residual performance to contamination.",
            "block_bbox": [
                124,
                1168,
                1064,
                1359
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    124.0,
                    1168.0
                ],
                [
                    1064.0,
